From: Zak Peirce <zak@orbis-win>
Subject: wine64: require the eboot release in relocatable builds

The relocatable PRX includes pw_relocated_wine_release_eboot(), but recent
hardware logs show the call is skipped: Oblivion then collides with the eboot
mapping at 0x400000. The weak symbol check can silently bypass this required
step even though the relocatable build always links app_entry.c, which defines
the function. Make the dependency strong and invoke it whenever a game is
selected. A missing implementation now fails at link time instead of producing
a package that appears relocatable but is not.

diff --git a/native/wine64_main.c b/native/wine64_main.c
--- a/native/wine64_main.c
+++ b/native/wine64_main.c
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@ extern int32_t sceGnmSubmitDone(void);
  * mapped at 0x400000-0x49c000 and sceKernelReserveVirtualRange returned
  * ENOMEM for every fixed low reservation -- measured by vmprobe/1045 as
  * `query ... name=executable hole=0`. FalloutNV.exe then relocated to
  * 0x530000 (it had loaded at 0x400000 on 19 consecutive pre-sync runs) and
  * Oblivion, which cannot relocate, died at STATUS_CONFLICTING_ADDRESSES. */
-extern int pw_relocated_wine_release_eboot(void) __attribute__((weak));
+extern int pw_relocated_wine_release_eboot(void);
 #endif
@@ -1563,7 +1563,7 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
 #ifdef PW_RELOCATABLE_WINE
     if (status == PW_OK) {
-        if (game && pw_relocated_wine_release_eboot) {
+        if (game) {
             int release_status = pw_relocated_wine_release_eboot();
             PS5LOG_LOG("PW_WINE64 relocatable eboot release status=%d", release_status);
             if (release_status) status = PW_ERR_STATE;
         }
